    "A variable is a place in the computer memory where some kind of value sometimes a string (some letters), sometimes a number is stored. Like the value 5 or the string \"hello”. A variable can have a simple name like just the letter x or any combination of letters like last_name or social_security_number, but not spaces, last name is not a valid variable name. If we have a variable called social_security_number we could assign it the value 123458789 as a sample social security number. Variables as their name implied can vary, which seems to be different to the mathematical view on variables, in math if you say x=5 then x is always equal to 5, but in programming we can say x=3 and then x=5 and the value would first be set to 3 and then change to 5(These are called assignments in programming). In the case of a string, the value between the double quotes will be assigned to the variable, for example if we had last_name we could assign \"Einstein” to it easily as follows: last_name= \"Einstein”.": [
      "A variable is a place in the computer memory where some kind of value sometimes a string (some letters), sometimes a number is stored. Like the value 5 or the string \"hello”. A variable can have a simple name like just the letter x or any combination of letters like last_name or social_security_number, but not spaces, last name is not a valid variable name. If we have a variable called social_security_number we could assign it the value 123458789 as a sample social security number. Variables as their name implied can vary, which seems to be different to the mathematical view on variables, in math if you say x=5 then x is always equal to 5, but in programming we can say x=3 and then x=5 and the value would first be set to 3 and then change to 5(These are called assignments in programming). In the case of a string, the value between the double quotes will be assigned to the variable, for example if we had last_name we could assign \"Einstein” to it easily as follows: last_name= \"Einstein”.",
      "変数とは、コンピューターのメモリの中で値を保存する場所です。値は文字列（文字の並び）のことも、数値のこともあります。たとえば、数値の5や文字列の\"hello\"です。変数名にはxのような1文字や、last_name、social_security_numberのような文字の組み合わせを使えますが、空白は使えないため、last nameは有効な変数名ではありません。social_security_numberという変数には、社会保障番号の例として123458789を代入できます。変数は、その名のとおり値を変えられます。数学でx=5と書いた場合は常にxが5を表すのに対し、プログラミングではx=3の後にx=5と書くと、値はまず3になり、その後5に変わります。これを「代入」と呼びます。文字列の場合は、二重引用符の間の文字列を変数に代入します。たとえばlast_nameに\"Einstein\"を代入するには、last_name = \"Einstein\"と書きます。",
      "Una variable es un lugar de la memoria del ordenador donde se guarda un valor, que puede ser una cadena de texto o un número; por ejemplo, el número 5 o la cadena \"hello\". Puede tener un nombre sencillo como x, o una combinación como last_name o social_security_number, pero no puede contener espacios: last name no es un nombre válido. A una variable llamada social_security_number podríamos asignarle 123458789 como ejemplo de número de seguridad social. Las variables, como su nombre indica, pueden cambiar. Esto difiere de la idea matemática: si en matemáticas decimos x=5, x mantiene ese valor; en programación podemos escribir x=3 y después x=5, de modo que primero vale 3 y luego 5. Estas operaciones se llaman asignaciones. En el caso de una cadena, se asigna a la variable el texto entre comillas dobles. Por ejemplo, podemos asignar \"Einstein\" a last_name escribiendo last_name = \"Einstein\"."
    ],
